{
  "claims": [
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "In a retrospective study of ultrasound-guided microwave ablation (MWA) for solid benign thyroid nodules (BTNs), 123 patients with 123 solid BTNs greater than 10 mL in size were treated between March 2019 and May 2024; the median initial nodule volume was 14.59 mL, final volume reduction rate (VRR) reached 80.73% over a mean follow-up of 28.32 ± 7.91 months, and 91.87% of patients achieved >50% VRR at the 12th month.",
      "cite": ["PMID:42003534"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E3", "E7"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "At the 12th month post-ablation, symptom scores in this cohort significantly decreased from 3.20 ± 2.75 to 0.76 ± 1.15 (p < 0.001) and cosmetic scores from 3.34 ± 0.85 to 1.97 ± 0.84 (p < 0.001), while the major complication rate was 4.88% with no cases of permanent sequelae.",
      "cite": ["PMID:42003534"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E8", "E9"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "No significant differences were observed in outcomes between high-risk surgical and low-risk surgical groups (VRR: 68.72% vs 69.96%, p = 0.57; complications: 26.67% vs 18.52%, p = 0.49), and baseline nodule volume showed a significant negative correlation with VRR (β = -0.82, p < 0.001).",
      "cite": ["PMID:42003534"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E10", "E11"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "In a retrospective evaluation of data from 73 patients who underwent MWA for benign thyroid nodules between September 2019 and May 2022, 24 patients (mean age 43.87 ± 9.5 years; 15 females, 62.5%) with solid or predominantly solid nodules who received a single session of cooled MWA (cMWA, n = 12) or uncooled MWA (uMWA, n = 12) were included.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41860416"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E16", "E17"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "Nodule volumes significantly decreased at the third and sixth month follow-ups, with similar volume reduction ratios between cMWA and uMWA (60.30 ± 8 vs 57.29 ± 8.2 at 3 months and 78.03 ± 6 vs 77.55 ± 10 at 6 months), whereas energy power and ablation duration differed significantly between groups (uMWA: 15 watts, 26.92 ± 4.9 min vs cMWA: 30 watts, 14.77 ± 4.79 min).",
      "cite": ["PMID:41860416"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E19", "E20"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "One major and 2 minor complications (nodule rupture and superficial hematoma) occurred only in the cMWA group, cosmetic concerns and pressure symptoms improved in both groups during follow-up, and the authors concluded that uMWA provided similar volume reduction to cooled systems and demonstrated a favorable safety profile, while the observed differences in complications require cautious interpretation because of the limited sample size and the inherent difference in power settings.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41860416"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E21", "E22", "E23"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C3",
      "text": "The two retrospective studies differ in population and follow-up, which constrains direct comparison of their MWA outcomes: the first included 123 patients with 123 solid BTNs greater than 10 mL with a mean follow-up of 28.32 ± 7.91 months and conventional ultrasound examinations at 1, 3, 6 and 12 months after ablation and annually thereafter, whereas the second analyzed 24 patients with solid or predominantly solid nodules who received a single session of cMWA (n = 12) or uMWA (n = 12) and reported nodule volume reductions at the third and sixth month follow-ups.",
      "cite": ["PMID:42003534", "PMID:41860416"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E3", "E4", "E7", "E17", "E19"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C3",
      "text": "In the comparative cooled versus uncooled study, energy power and ablation duration differed significantly between the cMWA and uMWA groups, and the authors state that the observed differences in complications require cautious interpretation because of the limited sample size and the inherent difference in power settings, indicating that the two groups were not matched on technical parameters.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41860416"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E20", "E23"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C3",
      "text": "Because both studies are retrospective and use different, non-overlapping patient sets and follow-up schedules, their reported MWA outcomes cannot be treated as directly comparable or as evidence that one ablation approach is superior to another.",
      "cite": ["PMID:42003534", "PMID:41860416"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E3", "E16"]
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "evidence_status": "complete",
  "evidence_gap": ""
}

